d by the Dashboard implementation. |\n40\t\n41\tNo other edges. There is exactly one code epic and one docs epic; no Investigations, onboarding, or external-dependency epics are warranted (see below).\n42\t\n43\t## Decomposition Analysis\n44\t\n45\t- **Step 1 (parse)**: The strategy uses a simplified format — Affected Components is a bare list (no change descriptions or owner teams), and there is no Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table, Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, or Non-Functional Requirements section. Recorded as health warnings; decomposition proceeds on available information. The Dashboard owner team (RHOAI Dashboard) is taken from the architecture context file; the Documentation owner team is inferred as RHOAI Documentation (no owner stated in the strategy).\n46\t- **Step 1.5 (Staff Engineer Input)**: Not present in the strategy.\n47\t- **Step 2 (component graph)**: Dashboard is an active component (code changes) → Implementation epic. Documentation is an active component (substantive content authoring) → `docs-authoring` epic. Component names are copied verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt` (`Dashboard`, `Documentation`) — no non-obvious mappings. Dashboard exists in architecture context (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`, GA, Konflux onboarded), so no onboarding chain is needed. Documentation has no architecture-context file; this does not trigger a provisioning chain because it is an existing Red Hat docs content process (routed via `docs-authoring`), not a new platform component being onboarded.\n48\t- **Step 3 (investigations)**: The strategy states \"None — the audit report enumerates the exact defects per flow.\" No open questions, risks, pending reviews, or conditional ADRs appear elsewhere in the strategy. No Investigation epics.\n49\t- **Step 4 (HLR mapping)**: HLR-1 (keyboard operability) and HLR-2 (consistent focus indicators) both target the same four audited flows in the same component and team — they edit the same UI surfaces and are consolidated into E001 (both P1, no priority split needed). HLR-3 (P2) maps to E002. Full coverage: HLR-1, HLR-2 → E001; HLR-3 → E002. No orphaned HLRs.\n50\t- **Priority inheritance**: E001 is P1 (highest of HLR-1/HLR-2). E002 is a `docs-authoring` epic — per the docs-authoring priority exception it does not push P1 upstream; its priority derives from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Normal` → P2, which also matches HLR-3's P2 label.\n51\t- **Tiered delivery**: Not applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing.\n52\t- **Rules 23–25**: Rule 23 (rollback/feature-flag) not applicable — the strategy fixes accessibility defects in existing flows, it does not replace an existing capability. Rule 24 (technical review AC) applied to E002, matching HLR-3's \"with technical review\" requirement. Rule 25: no `konflux-onboarding` epic exists in this decomposition (Dashboard is already Konflux-onboarded per architecture context), so the rule is not triggered; a build-pipeline-green AC is included on E001 as good practice.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n55\t\n56\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n57\t|-----|----------|-------------|---------|-------|\n58\t| HLR-1 | P1 | The four audited core flows are fully operable by keyboard, with no focus traps or unreachable controls. | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Core scope of the Dashboard implementation epic. |\n59\t| HLR-2 | P1 | Visible focus indicators are consistent across the audited flows. | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Consolidated with HLR-1: same component, team, and UI surfaces (the same four audited flows); HLR-2 is a presentation aspect of the same keyboard-operability fix pass — a split would double-touch the same components/styles. |\n60\t| HLR-3 | P2 | The accessibility conformance documentation (VPAT and product docs accessibility statement) is updated and republished to reflect the fixed state, with technical review. | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Distinct work stream (substantive content